WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:02.624
- [Protesters] Take off your riot gear!

00:00:02.624 --> 00:00:04.499
We don't see no riot here!

00:00:04.499 --> 00:00:06.630
Take off your riot gear!

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:08.520
- [Reporter] This protest
in Seattle, Washington

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.693
on June 1st looks to be contained,

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.793
but, in a split second,
everything unravels.

00:00:16.371 --> 00:00:18.882
(protesters screaming)

00:00:18.882 --> 00:00:21.882
(tear gas explodes)

00:00:24.813 --> 00:00:26.710
Since the killing of George Floyd,

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:28.610
police departments across the country

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:31.752
are facing increased
scrutiny over their tactics.

00:00:31.752 --> 00:00:33.306
(protesters shouting)

00:00:33.306 --> 00:00:35.803
- [Man] Keep the line, keep the line.

00:00:35.803 --> 00:00:36.636
- [Reporter] This incident

00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:38.480
in Seattle's Capitol Hill neighborhood

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.960
is under investigation by
the police department's

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.590
internal watchdog group for
the use of pepper spray,

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:46.543
blast balls and tear
gas to disperse a crowd.

00:00:46.543 --> 00:00:49.080
(tear gas explodes)

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.970
The Wall Street Journal analyzed
over two hours of footage

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:55.320
shot by multiple witnesses as
well as police scanner audio

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:59.340
from the scene to examine how
police and protester tactics

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.817
pushed the incident into chaos.

00:01:01.817 --> 00:01:02.703
- [Protesters] Black lives matter!

00:01:02.703 --> 00:01:05.060
Black lives matter!

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:06.990
- [Reporter] An hour
after a citywide curfew,

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:10.130
about 2,000 people are
headed east on Pine Street

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:13.229
toward the Seattle Police
East Precinct to protest.

00:01:13.229 --> 00:01:16.562
(Protestors protesting)

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.270
- [Reporter] But Seattle P.D. quickly

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:21.905
erect a barrier one block away.

00:01:21.905 --> 00:01:24.920
- [Policeman] The precinct's
pretty well cut off.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.290
- [Reporter] The scene
still seems under control.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:29.030
The precinct captain even listens

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:31.059
in response to protestor demands.

00:01:31.059 --> 00:01:32.360
- [Protestors] Let us through!

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:33.466
Let us through!

00:01:33.466 --> 00:01:34.660
Let us through!

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.900
- [Reporter] The tone changes
when an organizer warns

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.645
that some people have
other tactics in mind.

00:01:39.645 --> 00:01:41.522
- [Organizer] They're gonna try
and push through these gates

00:01:41.522 --> 00:01:42.780
and we're all gonna get hurt.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.050
- [Protestors] Let us walk!

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:45.391
Let us walk!

00:01:45.391 --> 00:01:46.391
Let us walk!

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:49.914
- [Policeman] We have a group
that pushed on the fence line

00:01:49.914 --> 00:01:51.503
and threw one bottle.

00:01:51.503 --> 00:01:54.640
At this point, they're loud
but we're under control.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:57.720
- [Reporter] Then, a crucial
change in place tactics.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.359
They swap the line of bike officers

00:01:59.359 --> 00:02:01.653
for officers in riot gear.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.070
They also discuss using pepper spray,

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:09.739
what they referred to as OC,
short for oleoresin capsicum.

00:02:09.739 --> 00:02:11.506
- [Policeman] We're not going
to let them push on the line

00:02:11.506 --> 00:02:13.684
because it creates an officer safety risk.

00:02:13.684 --> 00:02:15.158
If we get another significant push,

00:02:15.158 --> 00:02:16.740
we'll deploy OC.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.680
- [Reporter] Then, a change in
tactics from some protestors.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.150
A few at the front begin raising umbrellas

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:23.900
which protestors frequently use

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.001
to shield themselves from
pepper spray and tear gas.

00:02:27.001 --> 00:02:30.426
- [Policeman] We have several
people deploying umbrellas

00:02:30.426 --> 00:02:32.010
believed to be a deterrent.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:33.780
- [Reporter] Police
experts we spoke with note

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.170
that umbrellas are often seen as a hazard

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.100
because they block sight lines.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.640
Then, the pivotal moment.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:41.390
A person in front is holding an umbrella

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:43.830
that extends past the barricade.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:47.310
An officer reaches out attempting
to push it out of the way.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:50.310
(suspenseful music)

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:54.770
The officer grabs the umbrella

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:56.514
and the person tries to hold on.

00:02:56.514 --> 00:02:59.050
(suspenseful music)

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:00.000
At the same time,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.860
another officer deploys pepper spray

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.893
at the direction of the
incident commander or IC.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:09.000
- [Policeman] We're deploying
OC at the direction of the IC.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.000
(suspenseful music)

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:15.000
- [Reporter] Other officers follow suite,

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.203
spraying as the crowd backs away.

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:20.986
Some on the crowd throw
objects toward the police line.

00:03:20.986 --> 00:03:23.986
(suspenseful music)

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:28.860
Then, the order to deploy blast balls

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.160
and CS or tear gas.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.693
- [Policeman] Squad,
go ahead and deploy CS,

00:03:33.645 --> 00:03:35.332
blast balls and CS.

00:03:35.332 --> 00:03:38.332
(tear gas explodes)

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.490
- [Reporter] Most of the
protestors have dispersed

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.530
at this point but a few
remain close to the barrier

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:50.088
despite tear gas filling the air.

00:03:50.088 --> 00:03:52.506
(protestor wheezes)

00:03:52.506 --> 00:03:53.370
(suspenseful music)

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.600
Seattle Police Department policy dictates

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.290
that once a crowd poses an
imminent risk to public safety

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.320
or property destruction,

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.473
police should give an order to disperse.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:06.650
The policy also states officers
will not use blast balls

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:09.063
until a dispersal order has been issued.

00:04:10.029 --> 00:04:11.130
(tear gas explodes)

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:13.140
Footage we reviewed didn't show officers

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.049
giving a verbal order.

00:04:15.049 --> 00:04:16.800
(suspenseful music)

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.970
The Seattle Police
Department said the incident

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:21.560
is still under investigation.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.090
Police declare the June 1st protest a riot

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:28.673
because some people in the
crowd threw objects at police.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.970
11 people were arrested
according to Seattle Police.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:36.270
12 people required medical
attention, including police

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:37.570
and members of the public.

